MadGraph5_aMC@NLO
fNLO fixed-order process template

https://launchpad.net/mg5amcnlo

This process directory computes fixed-order LO and NLO cross sections
and distributions. It does not produce parton-level event samples.

Process information is available in index.html. Run settings are in
Cards/run_card.dat, and fixed-order histogram settings are in
Cards/FO_analyse_card.dat.

Decay-chain outputs also contain Cards/decay_card.dat. It sets the physical
total widths and an independent renormalization scale for each decaying
particle species. Full NLO production-and-decay bundles require both
LO_DECAY_WIDTH and NLO_DECAY_WIDTH for every NLO-corrected species. They use
the strict O(alpha_s) expansion

  dsigma = dsigma_LO + delta dsigma_prod
         + sum_i delta dsigma_decay_i
         - dsigma_LO sum_i (Gamma_NLO_i-Gamma_LO_i)/Gamma_LO_i.

Every term uses LO widths in all narrow-width denominators. The supplied
width differences are numerical O(alpha_s) inputs and are not multiplied by
another strong coupling. In the additive combination, products of production,
decay, and width corrections are excluded. The width sum runs over physical resonance occurrences,
including an occurrence whose selected partial decay has no QCD numerator
correction when the same particle species has an NLO total width. The
generated scales default to the corresponding particle masses. This is the
default ADDITIVE combination; `MULTIPLICATIVE = nlo_decay_combination` instead
forms a product of the selected LO/NLO block densities with the corresponding
LO/NLO total widths and includes products beyond strict NLO.
Density-matrix elements use the physical narrow-width factor 1/(2 m Gamma)
for each decay. Integration-channel weights use pole-free resonance currents,
so no auxiliary numerical width is introduced.
PRODUCTION_REN_SCALE_MOMENTA selects whether the production
renormalization scale uses the undecayed production momenta (CORE, the
default) or the fully decayed momenta (DECAYED). Production factorization and
Ellis-Sexton scales always use the production momenta.

For an additive full bundle, the final run directory contains
contribution_results_0.txt and contribution_results_1.txt. The latter is the
final integration result and resolves BORN, PRODUCTION_NLO, every labeled
DECAY_NLO term, WIDTH_COUNTERTERM, their sum, the ordinary total, and a
closure check. Each component carries its own Monte-Carlo error. These errors
are correlated because all components are evaluated on the same integration
points, so they must not be combined in quadrature to reconstruct the total
error.

A multiplicative bundle has no additive component decomposition and therefore
omits these contribution_results files. Its total and distributions are
reported through the ordinary fixed-order result and histogram files.

Mixed orders and decay-scale variations
----------------------

Decay cards use the MadGraph `value = parameter ! comment` style, without
a format/version field or an END record. To keep NLO corrections in only
part of an existing process, launch calculate_xsect NLO and set

  LO = production_order     ! LO production, selected decays remain NLO

or

  LO = decay_order          ! LO decays and widths, production remains NLO

Both settings default to NLO. An optional `LO = decay_perturbative_order(6)`
disables NLO for one absolute-PDG species, including its antiparticle and all
occurrences. The global decay_order=LO disables every decay correction. These
settings select from generated corrections; they cannot generate missing
ones. calculate_xsect LO always selects the all-LO calculation.

Set
DECAY_SCALE_VARIATION_MODE to CORRELATED to tie every decay scale factor to
the production renormalization-scale factor, or to INDEPENDENT to form the
Cartesian product of production muR, production muF, and one scale factor for
each distinct scale-dependent decay species. DECAY_SCALE_FACTORS must begin
with 1 and, in correlated mode, must equal rw_rscale in the same order.

For example, to vary decay scales independently of production:

  INDEPENDENT = decay_scale_variation_mode
  1.0, 2.0, 0.5 = decay_scale_factors

This works even with production reweight_scale disabled. The factor list
contains only scale factors, without a leading count. For each species the
generated decay_width_scale_mode is AUTO if its Born decay is independent of alpha_s,
and EXPLICIT otherwise. AUTO treats the supplied total widths as reference
values at decay_ren_scale(PDG) and uses

  Gamma_LO(mu) = Gamma_LO(ref),
  Gamma_NLO(mu) = Gamma_LO(ref)
      + [Gamma_NLO(ref)-Gamma_LO(ref)] alpha_s(mu)/alpha_s(ref).

This also requires an alpha_s-independent LO total width. EXPLICIT uses
width tables: every noncentral factor needs
lo_decay_width_variation(PDG, factor), and species kept at NLO also need
nlo_decay_width_variation(PDG, factor). These are physical TOTAL widths in
GeV, not partial widths. AUTO and explicit width tables cannot be combined
for the same species.

At a scale point k in an additive bundle,
the code evaluates

  R_LO(k) [ B(k) + delta P(k) + sum_i delta D_i(k) + C_Gamma(k) B(k) ],

where

  R_LO(k) = product_nodes Gamma_LO(central)/Gamma_LO(k),
  C_Gamma(k) = -sum_corrected_nodes
      [Gamma_NLO(k)-Gamma_LO(k)]/Gamma_LO(k).

Only the raw Born receives C_Gamma. R_LO consistently changes the LO
narrow-width denominators of every additive term. Thus a varied point still
contains exactly one NLO numerator correction at a time and never contains a
production-times-decay, decay-times-decay, or width-times-NLO correction.
Scale-point labels and the complete point set are written to the HwU weight
metadata and scale_pdf_dependence.dat.

Event-by-event decay scales are selected per species with
decay_dynamical_scale_choice(PDG): 0 uses the fixed decay_ren_scale, 1 sums
daughter transverse energies, 2 sums daughter transverse masses, and 3 uses
half the latter sum. The daughters are the immediate particles of that
decay block, including real radiation, in the event frame. Choice -1 calls
fixed_user_decay_scale in SubProcesses/dummy_fct.f90, which receives the
parent PDG, node number, block momenta (parent first), and reference scale.
Custom prescriptions must be infrared safe. Dynamic scales have a 2 GeV
floor before variation factors. They are permitted only for
alpha_s-independent Born decays with AUTO widths. Each resonance occurrence
has its own scale and running width, including when particle and antiparticle
share the same card entries. The width reference scale remains the fixed
decay_ren_scale value. Reweighting stores the scales of each event and
counterevent; multiplicative densities carry their own width normalization.

The run-card switch cut_decays defaults to false. With that setting, forced
decay products are omitted from built-in and user-defined generation cuts,
while fixed-order analyses still receive the complete decayed event. Set it
to true to apply the generation cuts to those products as well.

Factorized phase-space blocks
-----------------------------

Production and every decay node generate their phase space independently.
Each decay is sampled in its parent rest frame, records its own Jacobian,
phase-space weight and narrow-width normalization, and is then embedded by a
separate boost-only pass. A third pass materializes the canonical event for
cuts, scales and analyses. Matrix elements bypass that assembled event and
read only their boosted block-local momenta; subtraction kernels receive the
corresponding kernel-frame block.
Each matrix-element block returns a complex LO spin-density matrix plus, when
it owns the current NLO term, one separate real or virtual insertion matrix.
LO spectator matrices are cached against the boosted block-momentum revision.
The contraction accepts either all LO matrices or exactly one insertion, so
it cannot form production-times-decay or decay-times-decay corrections.
The block measures, active radiation map, production flux and incoming
integration weight are composed only at the integrator boundary. Production
and decay NLO terms also own separate adaptive grids for their three FKS
radiation variables.

calculate_xsect
---------------

Use bin/calculate_xsect to calculate the cross section and distributions:

  ./bin/calculate_xsect NLO

Replace NLO with LO for a leading-order computation. Results and
histograms are stored below Events/RUN. The supported analysis formats
are HwU and none.

aMCatNLO
--------

The bin/aMCatNLO executable opens the interactive command shell. Use
the calculate_xsect command from that shell for fixed-order runs.
